      "page_idx": 24,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.137,
            0.046,
            0.347,
            0.063
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 8"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.089,
            0.289,
            0.112
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Trapezium"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.12,
            0.577,
            0.232
          ],
          "content": "Parallelograms are quadrilaterals that have parallel opposite sides. We get a new type of quadrilateral if we relax this condition. **Trapezium:** A trapezium is a quadrilateral with at least one pair of parallel opposite sides."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.585,
            0.108,
            0.913,
            0.228
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_24_image_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.243,
            0.846,
            0.265
          ],
          "content": "？ Construct a trapezium. Measure the base angles (marked in the figure)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.277,
            0.757,
            0.324
          ],
          "content": "？ Can you find the remaining angles without measuring them? Since PQ||SR, we have"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.334,
            0.62,
            0.355
          ],
          "content": "**Property 1:** $\\angle S + \\angle P = 180^{\\circ}$ and $\\angle \\mathrm{R} + \\angle Q = 180^{\\circ}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.358,
            0.848,
            0.396
          ],
          "content": "Using these facts, the remaining angles can easily be found. Verify your answer after finding them."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.4,
            0.847,
            0.439
          ],
          "content": "When the non-parallel sides of a trapezium have the same lengths, the trapezium is called an **isosceles trapezium.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.448,
            0.605,
            0.471
          ],
          "content": "？ How do we construct an isosceles trapezium?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.483,
            0.831,
            0.507
          ],
          "content": "？ Construct an isosceles trapezium UVWX, with UV||XW. Measure ∠U."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.162,
            0.517,
            0.498,
            0.665
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_24_image_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.537,
            0.518,
            0.842,
            0.641
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_24_image_12.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.681,
            0.757,
            0.701
          ],
          "content": "Can you find the remaining angles without measuring them?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.705,
            0.847,
            0.762
          ],
          "content": "Does it appear that the angles opposite to the equal sides—∠U and ∠V—are also equal? Can we find congruent triangles here? Consider line segments XY and WZ perpendicular to UV."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.763,
            0.512,
            0.786
          ],
          "content": "What type of quadrilateral is XWZY?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.79,
            0.564,
            0.904
          ],
          "content": "Since XW||UV, $a = 180^{\\circ} - \\angle XYZ = 90^{\\circ},$ and $b = 180^{\\circ} - \\angle WZY  = 90^{\\circ}$ (since the internal angles on the same side of a transversal add up to 180°) Hence, XWZY is a rectangle."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.569,
            0.77,
            0.846,
            0.907
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_24_image_17.jpg"
        },
        {
